INT. WAY STATION - STAGE STOP - NIGHT

One lamp, one table. Four people circle a stew pot gone cold. Wind rattles the shutters.

MARGUERITE LAFLEUR, 40s, in a traveling dress, grips a carpetbag. HUDSON CROY, 30s, a drummer in a checked vest, drums his fingers. OLD PETE, 70, the station keeper, whittles.

MARGUERITE
I paid for a coach to Sedalia.

HUDSON
We all paid.

OLD PETE
Coach left this morning.

MARGUERITE
Without me?

OLD PETE
Without anyone. The driver took sick.

HUDSON
Then who's out there?

MARGUERITE
Where?

HUDSON
Listen.

Hoofbeats outside, slow. A rider dismounts. Boots on the porch.

OLD PETE
(under his breath)
Not tonight.

A VOICE (O.S.) outside, rough and unhurried.

DEPUTY KANE (O.S.)
Pete! Open up! I know you've got folks in there.

HUDSON
(whispering)
Is he the law?

OLD PETE
He is the law's cousin.

MARGUERITE
Then we open it.

HUDSON
We do not open it.

DEPUTY KANE (O.S.)
I'll count to five!

Hudson stands, walks to the door, and slides the bolt back. Marguerite and Pete stare. He opens it wide.
